@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Git vs TFVC";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="lead text-center">Understanding the differences between Git and Team Foundation Version Control (TFVC) to choose the right version control system for your project.</p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <div class="mt-4">
        <h2 class="text-success">Introduction</h2>
        <p>
            Azure Repos supports two types of version control: Git and TFVC. Both offer source code management, but they differ in architecture, workflow, and use cases.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/git-vs-tfvc.jpg" alt="Git vs TFVC Overview" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Comparing Git and TFVC for effective version control.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Key Differences -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-warning">Key Differences</h2>
        <table class="table table-bordered">
            <thead class="thead-dark">
                <tr>
                    <th>Feature</th>
                    <th>Git</th>
                    <th>TFVC</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Architecture</strong></td>
                    <td>Distributed version control system (DVCS)</td>
                    <td>Centralized version control system (CVCS)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Branching</strong></td>
                    <td>Lightweight and fast</td>
                    <td>Heavyweight and slower</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>History Tracking</strong></td>
                    <td>Full local history available</td>
                    <td>History is stored on a central server</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Offline Work</strong></td>
                    <td>Can work offline with full functionality</td>
                    <td>Requires internet connection to check in changes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Learning Curve</strong></td>
                    <td>Steeper learning curve due to distributed workflow</td>
                    <td>Easier for teams used to centralized control</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <!-- When to Use Git or TFVC -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-danger">When to Use Git or TFVC?</h2>
        <h4 class="text-info">Use Git if:</h4>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item">You need a distributed workflow for better collaboration.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">You want better branching and merging capabilities.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Your team prefers open-source tools and cross-platform support.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="text-info mt-4">Use TFVC if:</h4>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item">Your team is working on a large enterprise project with strict compliance needs.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">You require centralized control with granular permission settings.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Your organization is already using Azure DevOps and prefers a structured workflow.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/git-tfvc-usage.jpg" alt="Choosing between Git and TFVC" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Selecting the right version control system depends on project needs.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Conclusion -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-secondary">Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Git and TFVC both have their strengths, and the choice depends on your project requirements. While Git offers flexibility and is widely adopted in modern development, TFVC provides structured control suitable for enterprise applications.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/git-vs-tfvc-summary.jpg" alt="Git vs TFVC Summary" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Choosing the right version control system improves team productivity.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
